SOLUTIONS={
1:('FRAC-01','The flag has three equal vertical strips, two green and one white. Thus two of three equal parts are green: 2/3.'),
2:('ALG-04','Undo subtracting 5 by adding 5 to both sides. The missing number is 9 + 5 = 14. Check: 14 − 5 = 9.'),
3:('GEO-01','Add all four side lengths: 9 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 21 cm. Perimeter is the full boundary, not the area.'),
4:('DIG-01','All values are positive. Each step from 2 to 0.2 to 0.02 to 0.002 to 0.0002 divides by ten and makes the value smaller. Therefore 0.0002 is smallest.'),
5:('FRAC-01','The three fractions 1/2, 2/4 and 4/8 are equal. The expression is therefore 1/2 + 1/2 − 1/2 = 1/2.'),
6:('COUNT-06','Thirty cents cannot be made: a 50c coin is too much, and 20c coins give only multiples of 20. The other choices work as 50; 20+20+20; 20+20+20+20; and 50+20. So 30c is the impossible amount.'),
7:('GEO-04','The curved cut passes through all the layers of the flattened roll. Unrolling repeats the same outward-curved top edge across the width of the sheet. This gives the sequence of rounded humps in A, rather than inward scoops or a spiral cut.'),
8:('COUNT-01','Four students are ahead and twice four, or eight, are behind. Include Leo himself: 4 + 1 + 8 = 13 students.'),
9:('ALG-03','Work upward through the balanced hangers, treating the supports as weightless. Thyme balances 5 g of fennel, so thyme is also 5 g. Sage balances thyme plus fennel, so sage is 10 g. Mint balances the entire lower assembly: 5 + 5 + 10 = 20 g.'),
10:('FRAC-02','Use pears as one part. Nectarines are two parts, and apples are twice the nectarines, or four parts. There are 1 + 2 + 4 = 7 parts totalling 14 fruit, so each part is 2. Apples number 4 × 2 = 8.'),
11:('LOGIC-02','Cards dropped one after another must have a consistent bottom-to-top order: no card can be both above and below another through a cycle of overlaps. In E the visible top cards can be removed successively without such a contradiction (starting with the 6 of diamonds). The other pictures contain an impossible cycle in which a card would have to pass under and over the stack. Thus E could result from dropping the cards.'),
12:('GEO-01','Each square has side √25 = 5 cm. Joined side by side, they form a 10 by 5 cm rectangle. Its perimeter is 2(10 + 5) = 30 cm. The joined middle edge is inside and is not counted.'),
13:('RATE-04','On each paired hop the kangaroo gains 4 − 1 = 3 metres. To close a 42-metre gap requires 42 ÷ 3 = 14 hops.'),
14:('GEO-01','The vertical wire lengths are 8 cm upward, 8 + 3 = 11 cm downward and 3 cm upward: 22 cm altogether. All horizontal pieces run in the same direction and together span x. Thus x = 50 − 22 = 28 cm.'),
15:('RATE-01','Margie aims for a watch reading of 10:10, believing it means 10:00. Her watch is actually ten minutes slow, so the real time is 10:20: twenty minutes late. Rosie aims for a reading of 9:50, believing her watch is slow; because it is actually five minutes fast, the real time is 9:45, fifteen minutes early. Only Margie misses the train, by 20 minutes.'),
16:('SEQ-01','Hollow Square n has an n-by-n hole and an outer square of side n + 2 blocks. Its block count is (n + 2)² − n² = 4n + 4. For n = 7 this is 28 + 4 = 32. You can also extend the sequence 8,12,16,… by four each time.'),
17:('FRAC-01','The olive oil stays at 50 mL. If this is to be one-third of the new mixture, the total must be 3 × 50 = 150 mL. Starting from 100 mL, add 50 mL of vinegar.'),
18:('DIV-05','Remove complete 24-hour days: 2021 = 84 × 24 + 5. After 84 days the time is still 10 am; five more hours makes it 3 pm.'),
19:('DIG-05','The visible calculation is 3□ + □8 = 1□2. In the units column the hidden digit must be 4, since 4 + 8 = 12. In the tens column, 3 plus the hidden digit plus the carry 1 must reach at least 10. That hidden digit can be 6,7,8 or 9. The four completed sums are 34+68=102, 34+78=112, 34+88=122 and 34+98=132. There are 4 possibilities.'),
20:('COUNT-06','Three teams can hold at most 3 × 8 = 24 people, too few. Six teams require at least 6 × 6 = 36 people, too many. Four teams can accommodate some totals in the stated interval, and five can too. Therefore the possible numbers of teams range from 4 to 5.'),
21:('ALG-01','Let the winner have w votes. The other counts are w−31, w−25 and w−19. Their total is 4w − 75 = 453, so 4w = 528 and w = 132. Check: 132 + 101 + 107 + 113 = 453.'),
22:('SOLID-01','The left block’s top has area 27 and width 3, so the whole prism’s depth is 9 cm. Let the common height be h. The front-right block has width 42/h and depth 30/h, both integers, so h divides both 42 and 30: h is 1,2,3 or 6. Its depth is less than 9 because another block is behind it, so 30/h < 9; only h = 6 works. The right width is 42/6 = 7. The full prism measures (3+7) by 9 by 6, giving volume 540 cm³. Its rear top area checks as 7 × (9−5) = 28.'),
23:('DIV-05','A half-turn of the 10-tooth middle gear moves five teeth past each contact. Each 20-tooth outer gear therefore turns 5/20 = 1/4 of a revolution, in the opposite direction. Both large gears turn anticlockwise: A points left and the opening of C points up. The middle M is upside down. This is A.'),
24:('DIV-01','The completely green tiles form an a-by-b interior with ab = 20. One border tile is added around every edge, so the total is (a+2)(b+2). The factor pairs 1×20, 2×10 and 4×5 give totals 66,48 and 42. The largest is 66, from a long 1-by-20 green interior with a 3-by-22 outer frame.'),
25:('LOGIC-02','Read the five heights as 7,9,6,3,5, totalling 30. North and Central together must total 15; the only pair is 9 and 6. Central cannot be 9 because South must exceed it, so North is 9 and Central 6. South must then be the remaining value above 6: 7. East and West can be 3 and 5 in either order. Exactly three columns—North, Central and South—are certain.')}
